     1	# Lemma Bank
     2	
     3	## Lemma: Known-law conditional grid oracle
     4	Status: proved
     5	Assumptions: Finite horizon \(T\); exogenous/action-independent law on \(P_{1:T}\) known to the learner; the learner may depend on \(T\); comparator is \(\sup_a\mathbb E\sum_t r_t(a)\).
     6	Statement: For any \(K\ge1\), there is a deterministic horizon-aware learner with
     7	\[
     8	R_T\le T/K.
     9	\]
    10	In particular, \(K=T\) gives \(O(1)\) regret for every known exogenous law.
    11	Proof sketch: Let \(M_t=\max_{t<s\le T}P_s\), with empty maximum giving no trade. Given \(\mathcal F_t=\sigma(P_1,\ldots,P_t)\), define
    12	\[
    13	g_t(a)=\mathbb E[a{\bf 1}\{M_t>a\}\mid\mathcal F_t].
    14	\]
    15	Choose the smallest maximizer of \(g_t\) on \(\mathcal G_K=\{0,1/K,\ldots,(K-1)/K\}\). For any \(a\in[0,1]\), round down to \(b\in\mathcal G_K\) with \(b\le a\) and \(a-b\le1/K\), except \(a=1\) where the reward is zero. Pathwise,
    16	\[
    17	a{\bf 1}\{M_t>a\}\le b{\bf 1}\{M_t>b\}+1/K.
    18	\]
    19	Taking conditional expectations, summing over \(t\), and then taking \(\sup_a\) gives the bound.
    20	Known gap: This is an oracle/information-theoretic lemma, not a computational or unknown-law result. It is horizon-aware and does not imply an anytime policy.
