   149	## Lemma: Fixed-prior Bayes myopic optimality on a finite quote grid
   150	Status: proved
   151	Assumptions: Finite horizon \(T\); public exogenous prices; finite quote grid \(G\subset[0,1]\); prior \(\pi\) over exogenous laws; mixture law \(\bar P_\pi\); learner observes \(P_{1:t}\) before choosing \(a_t\); learner randomization is independent of the active law and does not affect prices or observations.
   152	Statement: With
   153	\[
   154	M_t=\max_{t<s\le T}P_s,\qquad
   155	g_t^\pi(h_t,a)=a\,\bar P_\pi(M_t>a\mid P_{1:t}=h_t),
   156	\]
   157	the optimal Bayes expected reward over grid-valued policies is
   158	\[
   159	\sup_A \mathbb E_{\bar P_\pi,A}\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a_t)
   160	=
   161	\sum_{t=1}^T
   162	\mathbb E_{\bar P_\pi}\left[\max_{a\in G}g_t^\pi(P_{1:t},a)\right].
   163	\]
   164	Proof sketch: Conditional on the pre-action sigma-field generated by \(P_{1:t}\), past actions, and private randomness, the conditional law of future public prices is the same as under \(\bar P_\pi(\cdot\mid P_{1:t})\). Thus any randomized action has conditional expected reward equal to a convex combination of \(g_t^\pi(P_{1:t},a)\) over \(a\in G\), bounded by the pointwise maximum. A grid-valued policy choosing a pointwise maximizer at every \(t\) attains the bound; finite \(G\) gives measurable selectors.
   165	Known gaps: The \([0,1]\) version needs suprema and measurable \(\varepsilon\)-selectors or a separate \(T/K\) grid approximation because strict thresholds can prevent maximizers.
   166	Source: `iterations/iter_004/idea_02/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_004/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   167	
   168	## Lemma: Finite reward-set minimax duality
   169	Status: proved
   170	Assumptions: Finite parameter set \(\Theta\); nonempty bounded convex attainable reward set \(C\subset\mathbb R^\Theta\); vector \(V\in\mathbb R^\Theta\).
   171	Statement:
   172	\[
   173	\inf_{x\in C}\max_{\theta\in\Theta}(V_\theta-x_\theta)
   174	=
   175	\sup_{\pi\in\Delta(\Theta)}
   176	\left[
   177	\sum_\theta\pi_\theta V_\theta-\sup_{x\in C}\sum_\theta\pi_\theta x_\theta
   178	\right].
   179	\]
   180	Proof sketch: Replace \(C\) by its compact convex closure \(\overline C\); continuity leaves the infimum and support functions unchanged. Since
   181	\[
   182	\max_\theta(V_\theta-x_\theta)=\max_{\pi\in\Delta(\Theta)}\sum_\theta\pi_\theta(V_\theta-x_\theta),
   183	\]
   184	finite-dimensional minimax/Sion for the continuous bilinear payoff on \(\overline C\times\Delta(\Theta)\) gives the identity.
   185	Source: `iterations/iter_005/idea_02/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_005/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`; synthesis.
   186	
   187	## Lemma: Corrected finite-grid minimax/Bayes-gap identity
   188	Status: proved conditional on the fixed-prior Bayes myopic lemma
   189	Assumptions: Finite law class \(\Theta\); finite quote grid \(G\); randomized measurable grid-valued policies; public exogenous prices; grid comparator
   190	\[
   191	V_\theta^G=\max_{a\in G}\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbb E_\theta r_t(a).
   192	\]
   193	Statement: Define
   194	\[
   195	\Psi_G(\pi)=
   196	\sum_{\theta}\pi_\theta V_\theta^G
   197	-
   198	\sum_{t=1}^T
   199	\mathbb E_{\bar P_\pi}\left[
   200	\max_{a\in G}a\,\bar P_\pi(M_t>a\mid P_{1:t})
   201	\right].
   202	\]
   203	Then
   204	\[
   205	\inf_A\sup_{\theta\in\Theta}
   206	\left(V_\theta^G-\mathbb E_\theta\sum_t r_t(a_t)\right)
   207	=
   208	\sup_{\pi\in\Delta(\Theta)}\Psi_G(\pi).
   209	\]
   210	Proof sketch: Let
   211	\[
   212	C=\{(L_\theta(A))_{\theta\in\Theta}:A\text{ admissible grid policy}\}.
   213	\]
   214	This set is convex by ex-ante randomization between policies and bounded in \([0,T]^\Theta\). Apply finite reward-set minimax duality with \(V=V^G\), then use fixed-prior Bayes myopic optimality to identify the support function:
   215	\[
   216	\sup_A\sum_\theta\pi_\theta L_\theta(A)
   217	=
   218	\sum_t\mathbb E_{\bar P_\pi}\max_{a\in G}
   219	a\,\bar P_\pi(M_t>a\mid P_{1:t}).
   220	\]
   221	Known gaps: The final write-up still needs to state the admissible policy class and public-exogenous model carefully. Cannot be stated with continuum \(V_\theta\) or continuum Bayes envelope without carrying discretization error. Randomized policies are essential; deterministic policies fail simple two-world tests. The identity is close to the minimax value and not a scalar tail characterization.
   222	Source: `iterations/iter_004/idea_01/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_004/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_005/idea_01/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_005/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_005/idea_02/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_005/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`; synthesis.
