   260	## Lemma: Infinite-class finite-subclass product-closure formula
   261	Status: proved
   262	Assumptions: Fixed finite horizon \(T\), fixed finite grid \(G\), public exogenous class \(\Theta\), grid comparator values \(V_\theta^G\), and attainable reward set
   263	\[
   264	C=\{(L_\theta(A))_{\theta\in\Theta}:A\text{ admissible randomized grid policy}\}\subset[0,T]^\Theta.
   265	\]
   266	Statement: Let
   267	\[
   268	\mathcal R_G(F)=\inf_{c\in C}\max_{\theta\in F}(V_\theta^G-c_\theta)
   269	\]
   270	for finite \(F\subset\Theta\), and
   271	\[
   272	\Phi_G(\Theta)=\sup_{F\Subset\Theta}\mathcal R_G(F).
   273	\]
   274	Then
   275	\[
   276	\Phi_G(\Theta)
   277	=
   278	\inf_{c\in\overline C^{pt}}\sup_{\theta\in\Theta}(V_\theta^G-c_\theta),
   279	\]
   280	where \(\overline C^{pt}\) is the product-topology closure of \(C\) in \([0,T]^\Theta\).
   281	Proof sketch: For \(\alpha>\Phi_G(\Theta)\), every finite set of closed constraints
   282	\[
   283	\{c\in\overline C^{pt}:V_\theta^G-c_\theta\le\alpha,\ \theta\in F\}
   284	\]
   285	is nonempty; compactness of \(\overline C^{pt}\) gives a point satisfying all constraints. Conversely, if a point in \(\overline C^{pt}\) controls all \(\theta\) at level \(\alpha\), product-topology approximation by points of \(C\) on a finite \(F\) gives \(\mathcal R_G(F)\le\alpha+\varepsilon\).
   286	Known gaps: This is the value of the product-closure relaxed game. It equals the true infinite-class minimax value only if \(C\) is product closed or another no-gap condition is proved.
   287	Source: `iterations/iter_007/idea_01/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_007/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   288	
   289	## Lemma: Product-closed no-gap criterion
   290	Status: proved
   291	Assumptions: Same fixed-grid setup as the product-closure formula.
   292	Statement: If \(C\) is product closed in \([0,T]^\Theta\), then
   293	\[
   294	\mathcal R_G(\Theta)=\Phi_G(\Theta).
   295	\]
   296	Proof sketch: Product closedness makes \(C=\overline C^{pt}\), so the product-closure formula becomes the true minimax value. Equivalently, finite-intersection compactness turns uniformly feasible finite-subclass controls into one reward vector controlling all worlds.
   297	Known gaps: Product closedness is not automatic for arbitrary infinite classes.
   298	Source: `iterations/iter_007/idea_01/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_007/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
