    14	## Arbitrary known finite-horizon exogenous laws refute fixed-law tail necessity
    15	Construction: Any law on \(P_{1:T}\) independent of learner actions, revealed to a horizon-aware learner through conditional probabilities.
    16	Facts: The learner can choose a grid maximizer of
    17	\[
    18	g_t(a)=\mathbb E[a{\bf 1}\{\max_{s>t}P_s>a\}\mid P_{1:t}]
    19	\]
    20	at each time. Downward grid rounding gives regret at most \(T/K\), hence \(O(1)\) with \(K=T\).
    21	Why it matters: This subsumes iid-uniform and terminal-spike singleton examples: no nontrivial raw-delay or truncation-tail condition can be necessary under these quantifiers.
