    46	## Adaptive known-law policies can have negative regret
    47	Construction: \(T=2\), grid \(G=\{0.5,0.9\}\). Let \(P_1\in\{0,1\}\) with probability \(1/2\) each; set \(P_2=0.6\) if \(P_1=0\), and \(P_2=1\) if \(P_1=1\). The learner knows the law and observes \(P_1\) before quoting.
    48	Facts: The best fixed grid quote has value \(0.5\). The adaptive known-law learner quotes \(0.5\) after \(P_1=0\) and \(0.9\) after \(P_1=1\), receiving expected reward \((0.5+0.9)/2=0.7\). Its regret against the best fixed quote is \(-0.2\).
    49	Why it matters: "Sublinear regret" should be stated as an upper-regret/positive-part requirement, not as absolute convergence of a minimax value to zero.
    50	Status: Valid sign-convention stress test.
    51	Source: `iterations/iter_004/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
    52	
    53	## Known terminal spike refutes necessity of \(\Delta\)
    54	Construction: Horizon \(T\). Let \(P_1=\cdots=P_{T-1}=0\), \(P_T\sim\mathrm{Unif}[0,1]\), and suppose the learner knows this singleton law.
    55	Facts: For each early quote, expected reward is \(a(1-a)\), maximized at \(a=1/2\). The learner can quote \(1/2\) and get zero regret. For any \(H=o(T)\), most early rewards are excluded by truncation, so \(\Delta_T(H)\approx (T-H)/4=\Omega(T)\).
    56	Why it matters: Large truncation loss can be harmless when the delayed reward curve is known/predictable.
    57	Status: Valid obstruction to \(\Delta\) as a necessary condition.
    58	Source: idea_01 explorer and critique.
    59	
    60	## Hidden-terminal minimax lower-bound instance
    61	Construction: Horizon \(T=D+1\). Two worlds share \(P_1=\cdots=P_D=0\). At terminal time,
    62	\[
    63	Y_L\sim\mathrm{Unif}[1/2,5/8],\qquad
    64	Y_H\sim\mathrm{Unif}[3/4,7/8].
    65	\]
    66	Facts: Before terminal time the learner cannot distinguish worlds. Per early quote \(a\), \(g_i(a)=a\Pr(Y_i>a)\) has \(V_L=1/2\), \(V_H=3/4\), while the best Bayes action under a uniform prior has value \(1/2\). Bayes gap is \(1/8\) per early round.
    67	Why it matters: Shows long delayed rewards can force \(\Omega(D)\) regret for a universal/minimax learner over an unknown class.
    68	Status: Valid finite-horizon class-level lower bound; not a fixed-law necessity result.
    69	Source: idea_02 explorer and critique.
    70	
    71	## q-scaled hidden-terminal lower-bound atom
    72	Construction: Horizon \(T=D+1\). Two worlds share \(P_1=\cdots=P_D=0\). At terminal time, world \(L\) uses \((1-q)\delta_0+q\,{\rm Unif}[1/2,5/8]\), while world \(H\) uses \((1-q)\delta_0+q\,{\rm Unif}[3/4,7/8]\). For infinite-process tail calculations, set \(P_s=0\) for \(s>T\).
    73	Facts: Every learner has regret at least \(qD/8\) on one of the two worlds. With absorbing extension, \(\beta(h)=q\) for \(h<D\) and \(0\) for \(h\ge D\). For integer \(H<D\),
    74	\[
    75	\Delta_L(H)=\delta_L(H)=q(D-H)/2,\qquad
    76	\Delta_H(H)=\delta_H(H)=3q(D-H)/4.
    77	\]
    78	Why it matters: This is the cleanest quantitative lower-bound atom linking valuable delayed tail mass and indistinguishability.
    79	Status: Valid finite-horizon minimax lower bound. It is not a singleton known-law obstruction, and by itself it does not match power-tail upper rates.
    80	Source: `iterations/iter_003/idea_02/explorer_01.md` and critique.
    81	
    82	## One-block power-tail lower-bound weakness
    83	Construction idea: Use the q-scaled atom under a nonincreasing tail envelope \(B\) by choosing \(q\le B(D)\).
    84	Facts: The resulting lower bound is only \(\Omega(D B(D))\). For \(B(D)=D^{-\alpha}\), this is \(\Omega(D^{1-\alpha})\), sublinear in the wrong exponent and non-growing for \(\alpha\ge1\).
    85	Why it matters: Shows the q-scaled atom is not enough for rate matching. Repetition must avoid iid block-splicing collapse and global-type one-reveal learning.
    86	Status: Valid obstruction to a proposed rate-matching route.
    87	Source: `iterations/iter_003/idea_02/explorer_01.md` and critique.
    88	
    89	## Rare-history essential-sup separation
    90	Construction: Draw \(N\ge1\) with \(\Pr(N=n)=3\cdot4^{-n}\). Choose distinct atoms \(x_n\in(0,1/4)\), set \(L_n=2^n\), and define the infinite public exogenous process
    91	\[
    92	P_1=x_N,\quad P_2=\cdots=P_{L_N+1}=0,\quad P_{L_N+2}=1,\quad P_t=0\text{ thereafter}.
    93	\]
    94	Let \(S_n=L_n+2\).
    95	Facts: For every finite \(h\), condition on the positive-probability atom \(P_1=x_n\) with \(2^n+1>h\). At \(t=1\), quote \(a=1/2\); then \(\tau_1(a)=2^n+1\) surely, so the infinite-process raw \(\beta(h)=1\). But
    96	\[
    97	V_T=\sup_a\mathbb E\sum_{t\le T}r_t(a)
    98	=\sum_{n:S_n\le T}3\cdot4^{-n}(S_n-1)\le4,
    99	\]
   100	and
   101	\[
   102	\Delta_T(H)=\delta_T(H)
   103	=\sum_{n:S_n\le T}3\cdot4^{-n}(S_n-1-H)_+
   104	\le C/H.
   105	\]
   106	Thus zero quoting has bounded expected regret even though raw essential-sup tails are maximally bad.
   107	Why it matters: This cleanly separates raw essential-sup delay probability from expected value-weighted truncation. The delayed spike has value \(1\); the obstruction is rarity of histories, not tiny payoff scale.
   108	Status: Valid expected-regret, singleton-law, infinite-process raw-\(\beta\) counterexample. Pathwise regret on rare atoms can be order \(2^n\), so this is not a high-probability or adversarial-path result.
   109	Source: `iterations/iter_007/idea_02/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_007/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   110	
   111	## Tiny-price long-delay blocks break raw beta without meaningful regret
   112	Construction: Infinite deterministic sequence. For \(m\ge1\), let \(L_m=2^m\), \(p_m=2^{-2m}\), and \(S_m=\sum_{j=1}^m(L_j+1)=2^{m+1}-2+m\). Block \(m\) has zeros at times \(S_{m-1}+1,\ldots,S_m-1\), then spike \(P_{S_m}=p_m\).
   113	Facts: For every finite \(h\), choose \(m\) with \(L_m>h\), start at the first zero of block \(m\), and quote \(a=p_m/2\). The first future price above \(a\) is the spike after \(L_m\) steps, so the infinite-process raw \(\beta(h)=1\). But for any horizon \(T\) and quote \(a\), let \(M=\max\{m:S_m\le T,\ p_m>a\}\), if nonempty. Since \(p_m\) decreases strictly, only quote times \(t<S_M\) can trade by horizon \(T\), hence
   114	\[
   115	\sum_{t\le T}r_t(a)=a(S_M-1)<p_M(S_M-1)
   116	=2^{-2M}(2^{M+1}+M-3)\le 1/2.
   117	\]
   118	If no such \(M\) exists, the reward is zero. Thus \(\sup_T\sup_a\sum_{t\le T}r_t(a)\le1/2\), and the zero-quote learner has regret at most \(1/2\) for every horizon.
   119	Iteration 7 strengthening: for integer \(H\),
   120	\[
   121	\sup_T\Delta_T(H)\le C/H.
   122	\]
   123	For \(a=0\) the loss is zero. For a fixed quote \(a>0\), let \(M=\max\{m:p_m>a\}\). Lost \(H\)-truncated reward is at most
   124	\[
   125	a\sum_{j\le M}(L_j+1-H)_+
   126	\le
   127	p_M\sum_{j\le M:L_j+1>H}(L_j+1)
   128	\le C\,4^{-M}2^M\le C/H,
   129	\]
   130	with zero loss if \(L_M+1\le H\).
   131	Why it matters: Raw hitting-delay probability ignores reward magnitude. Long delays can be certain only at quotes whose payoff scale is too small to create meaningful regret, and in fact value-weighted truncation is small.
   132	Status: Valid infinite-process/raw-\(\beta\) counterexample. For finite-horizon \(\beta_T\), state the result as failure of any uniform raw-tail necessity rather than \(\beta_T(h)=1\) for all \(h\).
   133	Source: `iterations/iter_006/solver_attempt.md`; `iterations/iter_006/idea_02/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_006/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_007/solver_attempt.md`; synthesis.
   134	
   135	## Infinite-class compactness/measurability gap
   136	Construction: Abstractly, take \(\Theta=\mathbb N\), \(V_\theta=1\), and attainable reward vectors
   137	\[
   138	C=\{c\in[0,1]^\mathbb N:c\text{ has finite support}\}.
   139	\]
   140	Every finite subclass can be controlled with value \(0\), but the full value is \(1\).
   141	Market-flavored stress test: Let \(A\subset[0,1]\) be non-Borel. With \(T=2\), \(G=\{1/3,2/3\}\), world \(x\) has \(P_1=x\) and \(P_2=1\) if \(x\in A\), \(P_2=1/2\) otherwise. Finite subclasses can be memorized by Borel policies, but a full-class Borel policy with regret \(<1/6\) would need to separate \(A\) by a Borel set.
   142	Iteration 9 linear amplification: For \(T=D+1\), set \(P_1=x\), \(P_2=\cdots=P_D=0\), and \(P_{D+1}=1\) on \(A\), \(P_{D+1}=1/2\) off \(A\). On \(G=\{1/3,2/3\}\), every finite subclass still has value \(0\), but the full Borel-policy value is \(D/6=(T-1)/6\). If \(q_t(x)\) is the Borel probability of quoting \(2/3\) on early round \(t\), then
   143	\[
   144	R_A(x)=\frac{D-\sum_t q_t(x)}3,\qquad
   145	R_{A^c}(x)=\frac{\sum_t q_t(x)}3.
   146	\]
   147	Regret \(<D/6\) everywhere would make \(A=\{x:D^{-1}\sum_tq_t(x)>1/2\}\) Borel, while \(q_t\equiv1/2\) attains \(D/6\).
   148	Why it matters: The finite-subclass Bayes modulus is not automatically a full infinite-class characterization. It characterizes the product-closure relaxation unless the attainable reward set is product closed.
   149	Status: Valid as a compactness/measurability obstruction. The market-flavored example is pathological and depends on Borel-policy restrictions; it disappears if arbitrary nonmeasurable policies are allowed or if \(A\) is Borel.
   150	Iteration 8 refinement: payoff-weighted reward-measure domination fails in the non-Borel label example because the relevant class contains uncountably many singular Dirac-type prefix/reward measures. Thus it remains a stress test for any unrestricted infinite-class theorem. Iteration 9 found no natural Borel-label version; identifiable Borel and countable-label variants are easy by direct decoding.
   151	Source: `iterations/iter_007/idea_01/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_007/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_008/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_009/idea_02/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_009/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   152	
   153	## Weak/Feller continuity is not a no-gap condition by itself
   154	Construction/stress test: Let \(\Theta=[0,1]\) and take deterministic prefixes \(P_1=\theta\). Such classes can be weakly compact and Feller-continuous while the prefix laws \(\{\delta_\theta\}\) are mutually singular and not total-variation separable.
   155	Facts: In the simple identifiable example \(T=2\), \(P_1=\theta\), \(P_2=1\), the class is easy for a Borel policy because the parameter is publicly revealed. However, finite interpolation can product-converge to arbitrary functions of \(\theta\), including non-Borel ones, so product-closedness of the Borel attainable reward set is not implied by weak/Feller continuity alone.
   156	Why it matters: Failure of product closedness and high regret are distinct. Identifiability may solve some nondominated classes, but it is a different mechanism from compactness and does not justify a general no-gap theorem.
   157	Status: Valid stress test against weak-continuity-only no-gap claims; not a lower-bound example by itself.
   158	Source: `iterations/iter_008/idea_02/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_008/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   159	
   160	## Action-dependent prices obstruct full-information reduction
   161	Construction idea: Let future prices depend on the learner's current or future quotes.
   162	Facts: Then counterfactual rewards \(r_t^H(b)\) for unplayed grid quotes may not be determined by the realized future price path, or may correspond to a different path under a comparator policy.
   163	Why it matters: The upper-bound theorem is not valid in this model without new causal definitions.
   164	Status: Model obstruction, not a numeric counterexample yet.
   165	Source: idea_01 critique.
   166	
   167	## Candidate anytime obstruction via two deadlines
   168	Construction idea: Deterministic known infinite sequence with super-growing blocks: \(N_m\) zeros, then price \(1/2\), then \(N_m\) zeros, then price \(1\), with \(N_m\) dominating all previous lengths.
   169	Facts sketched by critic: For the first \(N_m\) quotes of such a pair, the benchmark by the early deadline is about \(N_m/2\), while the benchmark by the later deadline is about \(N_m\). For any horizon-free quotes \(a_i\),
   170	\[
   171	A=N_m/2-\sum_i a_i{\bf 1}\{a_i<1/2\},\qquad
   172	B=N_m-\sum_i a_i
   173	\]
   174	satisfy \(A+B\ge N_m/2\), so one of the two deadlines has regret at least \(N_m/4\) from that block.
   175	Why it matters: The known-law oracle lemma depends on knowing the evaluation horizon. A single anytime policy may not inherit the \(O(1)\) guarantee.
   176	Status: Plausible stress test; needs a fully formal infinite-process and horizon-subsequence proof.
   177	Source: `iterations/iter_002/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   178	
   179	## Completed-kernel null-set stress test
   180	Construction idea: Let a completed-measurable finite-grid kernel differ from all Borel versions on a \(\lambda_t\)-null non-Borel set that some prefix law \(P^\theta_{1:t}\) may charge. If only payoff-weighted measures \(\mu^\theta_{t,a}\) are dominated by \(\lambda_t\), then the prefix law itself need not ignore that set.
   181	Facts: Borelizing the kernel can change the policy's behavior, future internal state, or pathwise feedback on such prefix histories. Under public exogenous prices and additive expected rewards, this is harmless for the no-gap theorem only because the reward coordinates use
   182	\[
   183	\int q_{t,a}\,d\mu^\theta_{t,a},
   184	\]
   185	and \(\mu^\theta_{t,a}\ll\lambda_t\) makes all changes on \(\lambda_t\)-null sets invisible to expected payoff.
   186	Why it matters: The iteration 10 Borel implementation lemma should be stated as reward-vector preservation, not behavioral equivalence. It also explains why exogeneity and additive rewards are essential: if actions affected future prices or private feedback carried extra information, changing behavior on a prefix-law-positive null set could matter.
   187	Status: Valid stress test for theorem wording; not a counterexample to payoff-weighted domination because the theorem only needs payoff-coordinate preservation.
   188	Source: `iterations/iter_010/idea_02/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_010/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_010/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   189	
   190	## Policy-alignment assumption stress tests
   191	Construction ideas:
   192	- Law-by-law policy versions: each singleton law may choose a convenient a.s. representative, but the representatives need not assemble into one common Borel policy on the full public history space.
   193	- Private side signal: two worlds have identical public price laws, but a private learner signal reveals \(\theta\) before decisions.
   194	- Law-correlated randomization: the learner's private seed is correlated with the active law.
   195	- Nonadditive/action-dependent market: current quotes affect future prices or rewards include inventory, budget, queue priority, or other cross-time state.
   196	
   197	Facts: In these variants, the one-time law-independent public-prefix marginal \(q_t(P_{1:t})\) may not exist or may not preserve expected reward coordinates. With a private side signal or law-correlated seed, action marginals conditional on \(P_{1:t}\) can depend on \(\theta\). With action-dependent prices or nonadditive rewards, a marginal at time \(t\) is insufficient because actions alter future paths or rewards.
   198	
   199	Why it matters: The iteration 12 forward alignment lemma is valid only for a common Borel finite-grid public-exogenous seed/state policy model with independent private randomness, full public observation, reconstructible feedback, and additive quote rewards.
   200	
   201	Status: Model-boundary obstructions, not counterexamples to the stated finite-grid public-exogenous alignment lemma.
   202	Source: `iterations/iter_012/idea_01/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_012/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.
   203	
   204	## Hidden/labeled tail-profile no-go pair
   205	Construction: Fix \(T=D+1\), \(q\in(0,1]\), and two terminal laws
   206	\[
   207	Y_L\sim(1-q)\delta_0+q\,{\rm Unif}[1/2,5/8],\qquad
   208	Y_H\sim(1-q)\delta_0+q\,{\rm Unif}[3/4,7/8].
   209	\]
   210	Hidden class:
   211	\[
   212	P_1=c,\quad P_2=\cdots=P_D=0,\quad P_{D+1}=Y_\theta.
   213	\]
   214	Labeled class:
   215	\[
   216	P_1=\ell_\theta,\quad P_2=\cdots=P_D=0,\quad P_{D+1}=Y_\theta,\qquad \ell_L\ne\ell_H.
   217	\]
   218	Facts: Under the stated protocol there is no quote before \(P_1\), and a quote at time \(t\) trades only against future prices \(P_s\), \(s>t\). Thus changing \(P_1\) to a label does not affect any legal quote reward or delay in a fixed world. The hidden and labeled versions have identical per-world reward curves, comparator values, \(\beta\), \(\Delta\), and \(\delta\). With absorbing prices after \(T\), \(\beta_\theta(h)=q\) for \(h<D\) and \(0\) for \(h\ge D\); for integer \(0\le H<D\),
   219	\[
   220	\Delta_L(H)=\delta_L(H)=q(D-H)/2,\qquad
   221	\Delta_H(H)=\delta_H(H)=3q(D-H)/4.
   222	\]
   223	However, the hidden class inherits the q-scaled hidden-terminal lower bound \(qD/8\), while in the labeled class the learner observes \(\theta\) before \(a_1\) and can play the active world's optimum. Continuum regret is zero; grid regret is zero if the grid contains \(1/2\) and \(3/4\), and otherwise only has the usual discretization loss.
   224	Why it matters: This refutes any unknown-class learnability criterion based only on individual laws' scalar delay/truncation profiles or their envelopes. The missing ingredient is public observability/predictability of the active reward curve.
   225	Status: Valid under public exogenous prices, no pre-\(P_1\) quote, and no same-period trade. It does not refute criteria involving posterior information, Bayes gaps, public-prefix laws, or the full statistical experiment.
   226	Source: `iterations/iter_011/solver_attempt.md`; `iterations/iter_011/idea_01/explorer_01.md`; `iterations/iter_011/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md`.

